In Ladybird, an inline box broken across lines with
box-decoration-break:slice draws its box shadow around one strip as long
as all its pieces placed end to end, and each piece shows its own slice,
cut at its break edges. Chrome 158, Firefox 151, and WebKit 26.5 render
the test that way, and a probe of inset, blurred, rounded, and several
shadows too, but for where Firefox places an inset shadow’s strip.

Gecko draws the shadow the same way: nsCSSRendering::GetShadowRect()
joins the pieces with JoinBoxesForSlice(). Blink and WebKit instead draw
each piece’s own shadow and leave out its sides at the breaks. Blink’s
PaintNormalBoxShadow() in InlineBoxFragmentPainterBase hands its
SidesToInclude() to BoxPainterBase, which clips with ClipToSides(). And
WebKit’s InlineBoxPainter::paintBoxShadow() hands its closed edges to
BackgroundPainter::paintBoxShadow().
